WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.722
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:03.722 --> 00:00:05.430
CLEMENTINE JACOBY:
Welcome to the session

00:00:05.430 --> 00:00:06.660
on real-world gaming.

00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:10.110
So my name is Clementine Jacoby,
and I'm a product manager

00:00:10.110 --> 00:00:11.730
on Google Maps.

00:00:11.730 --> 00:00:13.590
I'm actually based out
of Sydney, Australia

00:00:13.590 --> 00:00:16.884
which is where Google Maps
was born 13 years ago.

00:00:16.884 --> 00:00:18.300
But today, I'm
going to be talking

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:20.834
about real-world gaming.

00:00:20.834 --> 00:00:23.250
So first, I'm going to start
with a little bit of history.

00:00:23.250 --> 00:00:25.260
For those of you who
know Maps really well,

00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:27.750
you can just take
a quick power nap.

00:00:27.750 --> 00:00:29.880
But after that,
I'll show you some

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:33.360
of how mobile developers are
using location data to create

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:35.610
immersive experiences,
and then I'll

00:00:35.610 --> 00:00:37.860
dive into how you can get
started building games

00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:40.400
with real-world data.

00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:42.630
So let's dive in.

00:00:42.630 --> 00:00:45.630
Google Maps has been
building and understanding

00:00:45.630 --> 00:00:47.880
and expanding a
deep understanding

00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:50.750
of the world since 2004.

00:00:50.750 --> 00:00:52.760
And since 2005,
we've been making

00:00:52.760 --> 00:00:56.960
pieces of that model available
to developers like you.

00:00:56.960 --> 00:01:02.490
In fact, the Maps API was one
of Google's first APIs ever.

00:01:02.490 --> 00:01:05.200
So over the last 13 years,
our model of the world

00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:09.430
has evolved from providing
static maps to dynamic maps

00:01:09.430 --> 00:01:11.560
to then location intelligence.

00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:14.740
For those of you who saw
Gayathri's keynote yesterday,

00:01:14.740 --> 00:01:17.260
location intelligence
means delivering

00:01:17.260 --> 00:01:20.020
a rich, relevant,
realtime context

00:01:20.020 --> 00:01:22.650
about the world around the user.

00:01:22.650 --> 00:01:24.120
And through location
intelligence,

00:01:24.120 --> 00:01:26.640
developers are bringing
elements of the real world

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:28.950
into their applications.

00:01:28.950 --> 00:01:31.410
At the same time, they're
bringing their applications

00:01:31.410 --> 00:01:33.900
into the real world by
augmenting the world

00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:37.090
around their users
with digital content.

00:01:37.090 --> 00:01:39.830
As the augmented reality,
or AR, revolution

00:01:39.830 --> 00:01:43.370
begins to take shape,
we at Google Maps

00:01:43.370 --> 00:01:47.510
are seeing information,
imagery, and animations

00:01:47.510 --> 00:01:52.320
for mobile services
superimposed on the real world.

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:55.260
Developers can now
annotate real-world media

00:01:55.260 --> 00:01:58.290
to help people find places
faster or make informed

00:01:58.290 --> 00:02:02.170
decisions or just have fun.

00:02:02.170 --> 00:02:04.670
AR is enabled by two key trends.

00:02:04.670 --> 00:02:08.320
The first one is that super
powerful mobile devices

00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:10.810
are becoming more and more
prevalent and integrated

00:02:10.810 --> 00:02:13.010
into daily life.

00:02:13.010 --> 00:02:16.010
The second is that
advancing technology, things

00:02:16.010 --> 00:02:18.710
like location
precision, now allows

00:02:18.710 --> 00:02:21.980
us to place digital objects
in our real environment

00:02:21.980 --> 00:02:24.790
with increasing accuracy.

00:02:24.790 --> 00:02:28.450
So this goal of informing and
orienting and delighting users

00:02:28.450 --> 00:02:32.410
at a glance is now closer
than it's ever been.

00:02:32.410 --> 00:02:34.770
So how did we get here?

00:02:34.770 --> 00:02:36.630
The first thing is a good model.

00:02:36.630 --> 00:02:39.800
So over the last 13 years, we've
improved our model of the world

00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:44.070
by adding, again, more digital,
dynamic, and realtime content.

00:02:44.070 --> 00:02:46.400
The second piece is
better technology.

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:49.280
In those 13 years that we've
spent improving our model,

00:02:49.280 --> 00:02:51.450
technology has
also gotten better.

00:02:51.450 --> 00:02:53.630
And over time, combining
this accurate model

00:02:53.630 --> 00:02:56.780
with improving technology
has opened up new use cases

00:02:56.780 --> 00:02:59.370
for real-world information.

00:02:59.370 --> 00:03:01.370
This has led to
really big changes

00:03:01.370 --> 00:03:04.920
in how maps are
displayed and used.

00:03:04.920 --> 00:03:07.470
So let's look at
this progression.

00:03:07.470 --> 00:03:10.620
For hundreds of years, we
used paper maps to navigate.

00:03:10.620 --> 00:03:12.900
And then just 20 years
ago, AJAX brought maps

00:03:12.900 --> 00:03:15.700
to the digital world.

00:03:15.700 --> 00:03:18.640
This enabled the first-ever
Google Maps product,

00:03:18.640 --> 00:03:22.380
a map that could be explored
in your web browser.

00:03:22.380 --> 00:03:24.240
We designed our first
directions offering

00:03:24.240 --> 00:03:27.580
to help these desktop users plan
their journeys ahead of time.

00:03:30.150 --> 00:03:33.960
But then the mobile era ushered
in this huge paradigm shift

00:03:33.960 --> 00:03:35.610
for maps.

00:03:35.610 --> 00:03:37.720
So suddenly, users
were out walking around

00:03:37.720 --> 00:03:40.030
with their phones,
and they expected

00:03:40.030 --> 00:03:42.764
us to tell them where to go.

00:03:42.764 --> 00:03:44.430
They needed more than
just digital maps,

00:03:44.430 --> 00:03:47.100
they needed dynamic
maps that adjusted

00:03:47.100 --> 00:03:51.460
as they moved through the world
with their smartphone in hand.

00:03:51.460 --> 00:03:54.880
So as mobile took center
stage and adoption rates grew,

00:03:54.880 --> 00:03:57.790
demand shifted away
from route planning,

00:03:57.790 --> 00:04:01.070
and demand for on-the-go
navigation surged.

00:04:01.070 --> 00:04:03.770
As users came to rely
on us for directions,

00:04:03.770 --> 00:04:06.710
they did so even when they
were driving or biking,

00:04:06.710 --> 00:04:09.140
so we had to adapt our
offering to determine the best

00:04:09.140 --> 00:04:11.940
path no matter how
you were traveling.

00:04:11.940 --> 00:04:13.900
To do this, we
needed to learn way,

00:04:13.900 --> 00:04:16.769
way more about
real-world dynamics.

00:04:16.769 --> 00:04:18.420
Like how is the traffic?

00:04:18.420 --> 00:04:20.579
Were there accidents
along the route?

00:04:20.579 --> 00:04:23.550
What were the public
transit schedules?

00:04:23.550 --> 00:04:26.340
And we made all of this
dynamic information

00:04:26.340 --> 00:04:29.340
available via our
Directions API so

00:04:29.340 --> 00:04:33.140
that it could be used by
third party applications.

00:04:33.140 --> 00:04:36.410
Drivers were then able to use
that Directions API to optimize

00:04:36.410 --> 00:04:38.470
their routes around
passenger demand.

00:04:38.470 --> 00:04:40.887
And as location data
became more precise,

00:04:40.887 --> 00:04:42.470
ride sharing apps
started to flourish.

00:04:45.820 --> 00:04:49.030
Then with the advent of big
data and machine learning,

00:04:49.030 --> 00:04:51.310
we were able to move
again from dynamic maps

00:04:51.310 --> 00:04:54.520
to realtime location data.

00:04:54.520 --> 00:04:57.460
We now take an ever increasing
number of these realtime

00:04:57.460 --> 00:05:01.600
signals into account to provide
our users and a huge range

00:05:01.600 --> 00:05:05.170
of third party applications with
a more nuanced and intelligent

00:05:05.170 --> 00:05:06.130
model of the world.

00:05:09.540 --> 00:05:12.500
For example, rich
and reliable data

00:05:12.500 --> 00:05:15.650
coupled with powerful
machine learning algorithms

00:05:15.650 --> 00:05:19.500
allow us to help drivers
route around realtime traffic.

00:05:19.500 --> 00:05:20.970
And as of recently--

00:05:20.970 --> 00:05:23.190
and I think this one is
very cool for everyone

00:05:23.190 --> 00:05:24.894
who's from San Francisco--

00:05:24.894 --> 00:05:26.310
with the help of
machine learning,

00:05:26.310 --> 00:05:28.435
we can let you know if it
will be difficult to park

00:05:28.435 --> 00:05:31.710
at your destination right now.

00:05:31.710 --> 00:05:33.280
So as mentioned,
the canvas for all

00:05:33.280 --> 00:05:36.960
of this realtime information
is our model of the world.

00:05:36.960 --> 00:05:39.465
Advancing technology has enabled
us to go beyond the digital

00:05:39.465 --> 00:05:42.510
to dynamic to
realtime information.

00:05:42.510 --> 00:05:44.610
Meanwhile, that underlying
model of the world

00:05:44.610 --> 00:05:47.400
has become more
sophisticated, and it can now

00:05:47.400 --> 00:05:51.090
supply these realtime
services with all the detail

00:05:51.090 --> 00:05:53.490
that a developer
or our algorithms

00:05:53.490 --> 00:05:57.980
need to make
intelligent suggestions.

00:05:57.980 --> 00:06:00.830
So let's look at how that
underlying model has evolved

00:06:00.830 --> 00:06:02.990
to support new use cases.

00:06:02.990 --> 00:06:06.260
Our very first product,
that web-based digital map,

00:06:06.260 --> 00:06:09.510
used 2D imagery.

00:06:09.510 --> 00:06:11.910
We expanded on that
product and included things

00:06:11.910 --> 00:06:16.170
like 3D buildings and
elevation models and terrain.

00:06:16.170 --> 00:06:18.330
And then to usher
in the mobile era,

00:06:18.330 --> 00:06:19.770
we turned our
attention to helping

00:06:19.770 --> 00:06:24.850
users navigate urban
environments while on the go.

00:06:24.850 --> 00:06:27.550
Places play a really
big role here.

00:06:27.550 --> 00:06:31.540
Understanding cities is sort of
an obsession for Google Maps.

00:06:31.540 --> 00:06:35.020
Our maps now surface tons of
detail about real-world places

00:06:35.020 --> 00:06:38.170
to help users orient
themselves, but also to explore.

00:06:38.170 --> 00:06:42.070
So one of my favorite Google
Maps products is the Corridor.

00:06:42.070 --> 00:06:45.610
When I'm in a new city, I like
to know that one block over

00:06:45.610 --> 00:06:48.310
from me there are tons
of shops and restaurants,

00:06:48.310 --> 00:06:51.040
and I'm happy to walk a
block or two out of my way

00:06:51.040 --> 00:06:53.200
to go down a street
that's hopping,

00:06:53.200 --> 00:06:55.510
And Corridors let you do that.

00:06:55.510 --> 00:06:58.810
Our Places API is
this amazing resource

00:06:58.810 --> 00:07:00.460
that makes details like this--

00:07:00.460 --> 00:07:03.700
about 100 million points of
interest across the globe--

00:07:03.700 --> 00:07:06.000
available to developers.

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:07.420
And just like our
other services,

00:07:07.420 --> 00:07:09.470
the Places API is
constantly bringing

00:07:09.470 --> 00:07:14.510
in more and more dynamic and
realtime information sources--

00:07:14.510 --> 00:07:19.400
things like business' open
hours and ratings and reviews.

00:07:19.400 --> 00:07:23.360
And then finally, it turns out
that a lot of users like me get

00:07:23.360 --> 00:07:25.910
lost indoors, especially.

00:07:25.910 --> 00:07:27.890
So today, Google
Maps can even help

00:07:27.890 --> 00:07:31.550
you explore inside of
places like shopping malls

00:07:31.550 --> 00:07:33.097
and airports.

00:07:33.097 --> 00:07:34.680
This is a perfect
example, by the way,

00:07:34.680 --> 00:07:38.870
of advancing technology, like
indoor GPS and an improved

00:07:38.870 --> 00:07:41.240
model of the world like
indoor maps combining

00:07:41.240 --> 00:07:43.660
to create a new use case
for maps in the wild.

00:07:46.300 --> 00:07:48.550
OK, let's look at
one more example

00:07:48.550 --> 00:07:51.100
of a user experience where
I think our underlying data

00:07:51.100 --> 00:07:52.900
model really shines--

00:07:52.900 --> 00:07:54.700
going out to dinner.

00:07:54.700 --> 00:07:59.260
So today, Google Maps can help
you find a restaurant based

00:07:59.260 --> 00:08:04.660
on its location, ratings,
and reviews, read the menu,

00:08:04.660 --> 00:08:11.180
check how busy it will be now
and later tonight, book a table

00:08:11.180 --> 00:08:14.516
and hail a ride, all
from Google Maps.

00:08:14.516 --> 00:08:15.890
And to make this
possible, we had

00:08:15.890 --> 00:08:18.500
to bring together many
different signals about movement

00:08:18.500 --> 00:08:21.760
in the real world, including
how cars, trains, and crowds are

00:08:21.760 --> 00:08:22.260
moving.

00:08:24.954 --> 00:08:26.370
So this is what
it looks like when

00:08:26.370 --> 00:08:28.890
you bring this all together.

00:08:28.890 --> 00:08:30.660
At the bottom,
you've got technology

00:08:30.660 --> 00:08:34.289
steadily marching along,
improving and raising

00:08:34.289 --> 00:08:37.080
the bar for developers.

00:08:37.080 --> 00:08:38.760
And then riding
that wave, you have

00:08:38.760 --> 00:08:40.530
this increasingly
sophisticated model

00:08:40.530 --> 00:08:43.500
of the world with
more and more realtime

00:08:43.500 --> 00:08:46.650
information about the
global environment.

00:08:46.650 --> 00:08:50.370
And when we put them together,
new possibilities emerge.

00:08:50.370 --> 00:08:52.170
Services that ingest
this model become

00:08:52.170 --> 00:08:54.800
more relevant and intelligent.

00:08:54.800 --> 00:08:57.380
And critically,
those same services

00:08:57.380 --> 00:09:01.290
could be what power
your next game.

00:09:01.290 --> 00:09:02.940
So how are developers
like you using

00:09:02.940 --> 00:09:07.380
this model of the world
in your applications?

00:09:07.380 --> 00:09:11.100
Well, at Google, we're
seeing a big shift in demand

00:09:11.100 --> 00:09:14.700
from dynamic mapping solutions
like directions and place

00:09:14.700 --> 00:09:19.780
search to location intelligence.

00:09:19.780 --> 00:09:22.210
What is location intelligence?

00:09:22.210 --> 00:09:23.840
So it goes beyond the map.

00:09:23.840 --> 00:09:25.840
Location intelligence is
about bringing together

00:09:25.840 --> 00:09:28.180
everything we know about
the world around the user,

00:09:28.180 --> 00:09:30.070
about the context
around the user,

00:09:30.070 --> 00:09:33.560
and then delivering insights
when they're useful.

00:09:33.560 --> 00:09:35.849
Location intelligence,
for example,

00:09:35.849 --> 00:09:37.640
allows us to go beyond
knowing that there's

00:09:37.640 --> 00:09:41.510
a bus stop near you to knowing
the timetable for that bus stop

00:09:41.510 --> 00:09:45.530
to knowing how close the
next bus actually is.

00:09:45.530 --> 00:09:47.840
Or it means not just
telling you that there's

00:09:47.840 --> 00:09:51.026
a taco shop a block
away, but actually going

00:09:51.026 --> 00:09:53.150
a step further to tell you
that the tacos are cheap

00:09:53.150 --> 00:09:56.830
because we know you like cheap,
and that the shop is open now,

00:09:56.830 --> 00:09:59.090
and that it's not
even that crowded,

00:09:59.090 --> 00:10:01.810
and that the reviews
are fantastic.

00:10:01.810 --> 00:10:04.750
Understanding your users'
constantly changing environment

00:10:04.750 --> 00:10:09.760
is key to building experiences
that are relevant and personal.

00:10:09.760 --> 00:10:11.700
Personally, any taco
near me is to taco

00:10:11.700 --> 00:10:14.620
that I want to know about,
but that level of detail

00:10:14.620 --> 00:10:17.080
might be annoying or
bizarre or just irrelevant

00:10:17.080 --> 00:10:18.890
for someone else.

00:10:18.890 --> 00:10:23.160
So location intelligence--
it's powerful for developers

00:10:23.160 --> 00:10:25.740
because it allows you to
build relevant and rich

00:10:25.740 --> 00:10:29.760
experiences that cut across
industries, and in some cases

00:10:29.760 --> 00:10:31.440
even create new ones.

00:10:31.440 --> 00:10:34.920
For example, ride sharing.

00:10:34.920 --> 00:10:38.790
Not long ago, we stood on
street corners and hailed cabs.

00:10:38.790 --> 00:10:41.700
No location
intelligence involved.

00:10:41.700 --> 00:10:44.970
But now, we request a car
that arrives in moments.

00:10:44.970 --> 00:10:48.030
It's chosen based on
its proximity to you.

00:10:48.030 --> 00:10:50.640
And as it arrives,
we watch its approach

00:10:50.640 --> 00:10:53.299
in our app in real time.

00:10:53.299 --> 00:10:55.340
Your driver shows up
already knowing your desired

00:10:55.340 --> 00:10:59.090
destination, and all of
this is context specific

00:10:59.090 --> 00:11:01.150
to an individual user.

00:11:01.150 --> 00:11:03.640
This is location
intelligence at work.

00:11:03.640 --> 00:11:05.366
It's an experience,
and in this case,

00:11:05.366 --> 00:11:07.240
it's a whole industry
that just couldn't have

00:11:07.240 --> 00:11:11.230
existed without location data.

00:11:11.230 --> 00:11:14.550
I promise I'm getting to gaming.

00:11:14.550 --> 00:11:16.980
So in other cases, we
see location intelligence

00:11:16.980 --> 00:11:21.580
enhancing existing
industries, apps, or services.

00:11:21.580 --> 00:11:23.730
Mobile services
leverage location data

00:11:23.730 --> 00:11:26.790
to build experiences that
aren't really about maps,

00:11:26.790 --> 00:11:31.170
but they're tied to or fueled
by or mixed with the real world.

00:11:31.170 --> 00:11:33.630
One simple but very
powerful example of this

00:11:33.630 --> 00:11:35.710
is the check-in.

00:11:35.710 --> 00:11:38.270
So in basic terms,
what is the check-in?

00:11:38.270 --> 00:11:42.220
It's just a way of recording
a real-world action.

00:11:42.220 --> 00:11:45.790
But the applications of the
check-in have proven expansive.

00:11:45.790 --> 00:11:48.460
You can check-in to let your
friends know where you are,

00:11:48.460 --> 00:11:51.130
you can check-in to get
a deal at a real store,

00:11:51.130 --> 00:11:53.140
or even check-in
to enter a contest

00:11:53.140 --> 00:11:55.720
or rise on a leaderboard.

00:11:55.720 --> 00:11:57.210
And actually, the
leaderboard piece

00:11:57.210 --> 00:11:59.580
is interesting because
it demonstrates

00:11:59.580 --> 00:12:02.460
that a simple use
of location data

00:12:02.460 --> 00:12:07.110
is also a way of turning an
everyday action into a game--

00:12:07.110 --> 00:12:09.820
a game you might win by
rising on a leaderboard

00:12:09.820 --> 00:12:12.490
or getting from point
A to point B quickly

00:12:12.490 --> 00:12:16.730
or unlocking a special treasure
at an obscure location.

00:12:16.730 --> 00:12:20.750
So what we've noticed at Maps
is that integrating a location

00:12:20.750 --> 00:12:24.140
into your mobile service
often turns that experience

00:12:24.140 --> 00:12:26.050
into a game.

00:12:26.050 --> 00:12:28.120
Look at exercise.

00:12:28.120 --> 00:12:32.080
Exercise has proven itself
ripe for gamification.

00:12:32.080 --> 00:12:34.420
Tons of developers
are using location

00:12:34.420 --> 00:12:37.540
to augment their apps
and services in exercise.

00:12:37.540 --> 00:12:39.850
Today, you can use apps
to track your workouts,

00:12:39.850 --> 00:12:43.430
compete with friends,
log miles, earn trophies.

00:12:43.430 --> 00:12:48.920
Top brands are using location
data to gamify exercise.

00:12:48.920 --> 00:12:51.610
Points and milestones
and competitions

00:12:51.610 --> 00:12:57.100
are helping users get fit,
clear hurdles, stay motivated.

00:12:57.100 --> 00:12:58.990
And critically,
as developers use

00:12:58.990 --> 00:13:02.470
gamification to help
users achieve their goals,

00:13:02.470 --> 00:13:07.210
users respond by spending
more time in the app.

00:13:07.210 --> 00:13:11.570
So gamification
leads to engagement.

00:13:11.570 --> 00:13:14.600
In our API business,
we see brands competing

00:13:14.600 --> 00:13:17.150
to make their apps more
involving and immersive

00:13:17.150 --> 00:13:20.480
and compelling by bringing
in more and more location

00:13:20.480 --> 00:13:22.510
intelligence.

00:13:22.510 --> 00:13:24.040
They're using
location to motivate

00:13:24.040 --> 00:13:28.480
and to encourage competition
and to engage their users.

00:13:28.480 --> 00:13:31.750
And as that happens, the
very definition of a game

00:13:31.750 --> 00:13:35.020
has already begun to blur.

00:13:35.020 --> 00:13:37.436
Lots of real-world
experiences lend themselves

00:13:37.436 --> 00:13:39.310
well to gamification,
and I'm sure you've all

00:13:39.310 --> 00:13:40.510
seen it in your industries.

00:13:40.510 --> 00:13:41.980
We definitely have.

00:13:41.980 --> 00:13:44.740
Exploring a city is
kind of like a quest

00:13:44.740 --> 00:13:50.290
where you have to unlock certain
skills to achieve milestones.

00:13:50.290 --> 00:13:52.900
Finding a good meal is
always an adventure.

00:13:52.900 --> 00:13:55.270
So is trying to navigate
traffic with insider

00:13:55.270 --> 00:13:59.200
tips based on
realtime traffic jams.

00:13:59.200 --> 00:14:00.580
But these are all
implicit games,

00:14:00.580 --> 00:14:01.996
and what we're
starting to see now

00:14:01.996 --> 00:14:04.565
is that developers are making
these game-like aspects

00:14:04.565 --> 00:14:05.065
explicit.

00:14:08.730 --> 00:14:10.320
So we've talked
about two trends,

00:14:10.320 --> 00:14:13.350
and they're unfolding,
really, in parallel.

00:14:13.350 --> 00:14:17.590
First, as I illustrated
earlier, as devices improve

00:14:17.590 --> 00:14:19.230
and our model of
the world becomes

00:14:19.230 --> 00:14:21.720
more nuanced and
precise, developers

00:14:21.720 --> 00:14:25.230
are suddenly able to augment
the world with digital content

00:14:25.230 --> 00:14:28.940
in ways that just
weren't possible before.

00:14:28.940 --> 00:14:31.120
And secondly, as
powerful devices

00:14:31.120 --> 00:14:33.910
become more and more
integrated into life,

00:14:33.910 --> 00:14:37.090
standards for what constitutes
an engaging mobile experience

00:14:37.090 --> 00:14:39.230
just keep rising.

00:14:39.230 --> 00:14:41.600
This is causing a shift
that's bringing routine tasks

00:14:41.600 --> 00:14:45.220
and fun closer together
through gamification,

00:14:45.220 --> 00:14:48.680
and game developers find
themselves right at the nexus

00:14:48.680 --> 00:14:52.450
point of these two trends.

00:14:52.450 --> 00:14:55.810
And boy are game developers
rising to the occasion.

00:14:55.810 --> 00:14:59.260
So let's look at an example of
a developer, a game developer,

00:14:59.260 --> 00:15:03.370
using location data, powerful
smartphone penetration,

00:15:03.370 --> 00:15:05.770
and innovative
technology to create

00:15:05.770 --> 00:15:10.720
a phenomenon that would not have
been possible a few years ago.

00:15:10.720 --> 00:15:15.930
Last year, we saw real-world
gaming go mainstream overnight.

00:15:15.930 --> 00:15:20.430
On July 5th, a company named
Niantic launched Pokemon Go,

00:15:20.430 --> 00:15:23.130
and by the morning of July
6th, people of all ages

00:15:23.130 --> 00:15:28.630
were outside walking around,
collecting virtual Pokemon.

00:15:28.630 --> 00:15:30.890
This game was different from
any that had come before,

00:15:30.890 --> 00:15:33.710
and you could see
it on the streets.

00:15:33.710 --> 00:15:36.830
But real-world gaming
is not a new idea.

00:15:36.830 --> 00:15:39.230
We've been building games
based on real-world discovery

00:15:39.230 --> 00:15:40.610
for a long time.

00:15:40.610 --> 00:15:45.170
Think of geocaching or scavenger
hunts or Niantic's first game

00:15:45.170 --> 00:15:49.200
that they built within
Google, Ingress.

00:15:49.200 --> 00:15:51.870
But the difference now
is that mobile phones

00:15:51.870 --> 00:15:55.560
are advanced enough
and ubiquitous enough

00:15:55.560 --> 00:15:59.490
that real-world games have
a massive potential market

00:15:59.490 --> 00:16:00.990
with all of the
technology that they

00:16:00.990 --> 00:16:03.599
need to play in their pockets.

00:16:03.599 --> 00:16:05.640
The difference now is that
our model of the world

00:16:05.640 --> 00:16:10.520
is so detailed that it can be
used to build experiences that

00:16:10.520 --> 00:16:13.850
leverage this complexity
and dynamism in ways

00:16:13.850 --> 00:16:17.014
that weren't possible before.

00:16:17.014 --> 00:16:18.430
So we're arriving
in a place where

00:16:18.430 --> 00:16:22.260
it's possible to augment the
world with mobile content

00:16:22.260 --> 00:16:24.490
and to augment
mobile experiences

00:16:24.490 --> 00:16:26.950
with real-world
content, and this just

00:16:26.950 --> 00:16:29.765
raises the bar, again, for what
highly engaged users will soon

00:16:29.765 --> 00:16:30.265
demand.

00:16:33.050 --> 00:16:35.180
The colossal growth
of Pokemon Go

00:16:35.180 --> 00:16:38.620
confirmed that we've arrived
at an inflection point--

00:16:38.620 --> 00:16:41.500
a time when a sophisticated
model of the real world

00:16:41.500 --> 00:16:45.790
combined with powerful IP and
an unprecedented game mechanic

00:16:45.790 --> 00:16:49.120
can be leveraged by smartphones
to blend mobile experiences

00:16:49.120 --> 00:16:52.600
with the real world.

00:16:52.600 --> 00:16:54.940
The launch of Pokemon Go
was a global phenomenon

00:16:54.940 --> 00:16:57.490
not only for its unique
gaming experience,

00:16:57.490 --> 00:17:01.290
but also because of
its explosive growth.

00:17:01.290 --> 00:17:03.850
Niantic and engineers
from Google Cloud

00:17:03.850 --> 00:17:06.730
worked together to scale
to 50 times Niantic's

00:17:06.730 --> 00:17:09.099
forecasted traffic
to accommodate

00:17:09.099 --> 00:17:12.900
their unexpected
popularity at launch.

00:17:12.900 --> 00:17:14.650
Niantic set out to
create a different kind

00:17:14.650 --> 00:17:16.930
of gaming experience, as well--

00:17:16.930 --> 00:17:18.910
one that got participants
off of their couch

00:17:18.910 --> 00:17:21.010
and into the real world.

00:17:21.010 --> 00:17:23.800
They leveraged Google
Maps, among other sources,

00:17:23.800 --> 00:17:29.050
to create a truly iconic
augmented reality game.

00:17:29.050 --> 00:17:30.970
So let's take a
closer look at some

00:17:30.970 --> 00:17:32.620
of the technologies
and data that

00:17:32.620 --> 00:17:35.020
are used by real-world games.

00:17:35.020 --> 00:17:37.150
This is not specific
to any particular game,

00:17:37.150 --> 00:17:38.860
and the details
will vary depending

00:17:38.860 --> 00:17:40.492
on what you're trying to build.

00:17:40.492 --> 00:17:41.950
But at a high level,
these are some

00:17:41.950 --> 00:17:43.990
of the features you'll need.

00:17:43.990 --> 00:17:47.560
First, precise user location.

00:17:47.560 --> 00:17:49.960
Location precision has
improved dramatically

00:17:49.960 --> 00:17:53.200
in the last couple of
years, and it continues to.

00:17:53.200 --> 00:17:56.290
It's now possible to tell not
just what city block you're on,

00:17:56.290 --> 00:17:57.910
but what store
you're in front of,

00:17:57.910 --> 00:18:00.730
and even what you're looking
at, or what direction

00:18:00.730 --> 00:18:02.400
you're facing.

00:18:02.400 --> 00:18:05.710
And for developers, this means a
more fine-grained understanding

00:18:05.710 --> 00:18:10.650
of exactly where their users are
in relation to in-game objects.

00:18:10.650 --> 00:18:12.960
Real-world games can
leverage recent improvements

00:18:12.960 --> 00:18:15.120
in location
precision to pinpoint

00:18:15.120 --> 00:18:18.049
exactly where its players
are and, in some cases, what

00:18:18.049 --> 00:18:18.840
they're looking at.

00:18:21.450 --> 00:18:23.890
Next, base maps.

00:18:23.890 --> 00:18:27.160
To build a real-world
game, you need a map

00:18:27.160 --> 00:18:29.550
that reflects the real world.

00:18:29.550 --> 00:18:32.730
You need the geometry that
defines roads and paths

00:18:32.730 --> 00:18:34.710
that users walk along.

00:18:34.710 --> 00:18:37.800
You need polygons that define
the building footprints

00:18:37.800 --> 00:18:39.500
around them.

00:18:39.500 --> 00:18:42.614
You need the outlines of
parks and lakes and fields

00:18:42.614 --> 00:18:44.780
and the metadata that helps
you actually distinguish

00:18:44.780 --> 00:18:46.860
between those things.

00:18:46.860 --> 00:18:49.830
You don't want to place objects
in the middle of a large body

00:18:49.830 --> 00:18:51.360
of water because
you thought it was

00:18:51.360 --> 00:18:56.640
a park, or style lake the
same way you would style park.

00:18:56.640 --> 00:19:00.130
In a real-world game, a style
based map is your game level,

00:19:00.130 --> 00:19:02.100
and there are lots of
choices for the developer

00:19:02.100 --> 00:19:04.290
and the artist to make.

00:19:04.290 --> 00:19:07.590
What level of visual interest
do you want on the map?

00:19:07.590 --> 00:19:11.010
Where do you want to focus
your users' attention?

00:19:11.010 --> 00:19:13.350
For example, you might use
a full 3D building model

00:19:13.350 --> 00:19:15.660
to create an
immersive experience,

00:19:15.660 --> 00:19:18.900
or instead, you could use
simple building footprints

00:19:18.900 --> 00:19:20.910
to orient users without
overwhelming them

00:19:20.910 --> 00:19:23.710
with visual detail.

00:19:23.710 --> 00:19:25.630
There are a lot of
ways to use geometry

00:19:25.630 --> 00:19:27.550
and the metadata of map
features to determine

00:19:27.550 --> 00:19:29.580
how to style a game world.

00:19:29.580 --> 00:19:32.980
And you can also use the
base map to design gameplay,

00:19:32.980 --> 00:19:34.670
and this is a bit different.

00:19:34.670 --> 00:19:36.790
So here, you might use
the type of an area

00:19:36.790 --> 00:19:39.400
to determine what
kind of game object

00:19:39.400 --> 00:19:42.080
would be collected there.

00:19:42.080 --> 00:19:44.090
For example, you could
use a map to determine

00:19:44.090 --> 00:19:46.310
where the forests
are, and allow users

00:19:46.310 --> 00:19:49.630
to go there to collect wood.

00:19:49.630 --> 00:19:52.230
Finally, global scale.

00:19:52.230 --> 00:19:55.080
With Google Maps global
rollout of a game

00:19:55.080 --> 00:19:57.510
can take place
seamlessly because you

00:19:57.510 --> 00:20:00.510
can leverage the infinite
variation of the real world

00:20:00.510 --> 00:20:02.544
to make your game
nuanced and interesting

00:20:02.544 --> 00:20:03.960
no matter where
it's being played.

00:20:08.110 --> 00:20:10.650
So let's focus on
just the maps piece.

00:20:10.650 --> 00:20:15.180
How could you use our maps
to create a real-world game?

00:20:15.180 --> 00:20:18.240
At a very, very high
level, it looks like this.

00:20:18.240 --> 00:20:21.876
First, you request map
tiles from Google Maps.

00:20:21.876 --> 00:20:23.250
Then you filter
all the data that

00:20:23.250 --> 00:20:24.640
isn't critical to your game.

00:20:24.640 --> 00:20:28.020
So for example, maybe you don't
care about road labels or road

00:20:28.020 --> 00:20:32.490
shields or cycling lanes because
you expect all your users

00:20:32.490 --> 00:20:34.580
to be walking.

00:20:34.580 --> 00:20:36.650
Next, you would style
the map to suit the look

00:20:36.650 --> 00:20:38.180
and feel of your game.

00:20:38.180 --> 00:20:41.630
For example, you could make
pedestrian paths and boardwalks

00:20:41.630 --> 00:20:44.540
and beaches really broad
and visually dominant,

00:20:44.540 --> 00:20:46.850
and you could de-emphasize
highways and freeways

00:20:46.850 --> 00:20:48.950
and other places that you
didn't want to encourage

00:20:48.950 --> 00:20:51.840
your users to walk.

00:20:51.840 --> 00:20:53.610
You can apply your own
styling to any area

00:20:53.610 --> 00:20:56.880
on the map using the underlying
metadata to determine what type

00:20:56.880 --> 00:20:58.710
of area it is.

00:20:58.710 --> 00:21:01.980
The result is a look and
feel that's all your own.

00:21:01.980 --> 00:21:04.020
And finally, you add your game.

00:21:04.020 --> 00:21:05.820
The map is just the foundation.

00:21:05.820 --> 00:21:08.640
Now you add the characters,
landmarks, and interactions

00:21:08.640 --> 00:21:09.530
that drive gameplay.

00:21:12.400 --> 00:21:14.320
That's just a
high-level overview,

00:21:14.320 --> 00:21:17.290
and now I want to use an example
to dive a bit deeper into how

00:21:17.290 --> 00:21:20.116
you can use geodata to build
real-world games, and some

00:21:20.116 --> 00:21:21.490
of the considerations
that you'll

00:21:21.490 --> 00:21:23.850
encounter as a developer.

00:21:23.850 --> 00:21:26.110
So for the purposes
of this exercise,

00:21:26.110 --> 00:21:28.890
let's imagine that you want
to build a real-world version

00:21:28.890 --> 00:21:31.200
of a resource-gathering game.

00:21:31.200 --> 00:21:33.630
So in this game,
players will move

00:21:33.630 --> 00:21:37.440
around collecting resources
and claiming land and building

00:21:37.440 --> 00:21:41.240
kingdoms and forming
alliances, and so forth.

00:21:41.240 --> 00:21:43.320
The variations on this
theme are infinite,

00:21:43.320 --> 00:21:45.860
and it lends itself well to a
location-based gaming example

00:21:45.860 --> 00:21:47.502
because there's lots
of different types

00:21:47.502 --> 00:21:49.460
of real-world data that
you'll want to consider

00:21:49.460 --> 00:21:52.184
when you're building it.

00:21:52.184 --> 00:21:54.100
One of the very first
things you'll want to do

00:21:54.100 --> 00:21:56.290
is link valuable resources--

00:21:56.290 --> 00:21:59.420
things like iron
and wood and water--

00:21:59.420 --> 00:22:02.530
onto real-world locations
like restaurants and schools

00:22:02.530 --> 00:22:05.440
and grocery stores.

00:22:05.440 --> 00:22:07.180
To do this, you
need to understand

00:22:07.180 --> 00:22:09.820
what types of businesses are
within reachable distance

00:22:09.820 --> 00:22:12.930
of the user.

00:22:12.930 --> 00:22:15.180
You'll also need to know
properties of the businesses

00:22:15.180 --> 00:22:16.710
in a given area.

00:22:16.710 --> 00:22:18.480
For example, maybe
you want to identify

00:22:18.480 --> 00:22:21.570
all of the real-world banks
and allow your players

00:22:21.570 --> 00:22:25.550
to collect in-game currency
at those locations.

00:22:25.550 --> 00:22:28.940
Or maybe you want to map
a rare type of business

00:22:28.940 --> 00:22:31.211
to a rare resource.

00:22:31.211 --> 00:22:33.460
Maybe some treasures are so
special that they can only

00:22:33.460 --> 00:22:36.310
be collected at a single
landmark per city,

00:22:36.310 --> 00:22:39.580
and others are much more common,
like collecting provisions

00:22:39.580 --> 00:22:42.400
at a grocery store.

00:22:42.400 --> 00:22:44.400
Maybe some resources
move around like herds

00:22:44.400 --> 00:22:49.600
of animals and others are
fixed in static locations.

00:22:49.600 --> 00:22:51.780
Let's say you've done
that, and after linking

00:22:51.780 --> 00:22:54.600
real-world locations
to in-game resources,

00:22:54.600 --> 00:22:56.580
you turn your attention
to understanding

00:22:56.580 --> 00:22:59.190
how your game is
driving foot traffic,

00:22:59.190 --> 00:23:02.640
and how much foot
traffic it's driving.

00:23:02.640 --> 00:23:04.970
You'll want to know
where crowds will be so

00:23:04.970 --> 00:23:08.250
that you can generate more
resources in crowded areas.

00:23:08.250 --> 00:23:10.080
Otherwise the resources
will dry up quickly

00:23:10.080 --> 00:23:11.460
and your game will be no fun.

00:23:11.460 --> 00:23:14.280
Your players can't progress.

00:23:14.280 --> 00:23:16.890
You'll want to understand how
player behavior downtown is

00:23:16.890 --> 00:23:20.550
different from player
behavior in the suburbs.

00:23:20.550 --> 00:23:22.540
And if your game is
played all over the world,

00:23:22.540 --> 00:23:24.040
you'll probably
need Cloud Analytics

00:23:24.040 --> 00:23:26.800
to find these patterns--

00:23:26.800 --> 00:23:29.350
how the game is being played
at night versus in the day,

00:23:29.350 --> 00:23:32.680
and rain versus shine,
or even in the northern

00:23:32.680 --> 00:23:34.390
versus the southern hemisphere.

00:23:34.390 --> 00:23:38.202
All of these things make a
difference to your players.

00:23:38.202 --> 00:23:39.910
Once you find these
patterns, though, you

00:23:39.910 --> 00:23:42.560
can adjust your
game accordingly.

00:23:42.560 --> 00:23:44.830
For example, in Sydney
where the weather's perfect,

00:23:44.830 --> 00:23:47.530
it makes sense to place
resources in parks.

00:23:47.530 --> 00:23:49.642
But in New York City
during the winter,

00:23:49.642 --> 00:23:51.975
you probably want to place
resources in subway stations.

00:23:55.070 --> 00:23:58.010
Then with a better understanding
of how your game drives

00:23:58.010 --> 00:24:01.220
foot traffic, you could consider
how to thoughtfully connect

00:24:01.220 --> 00:24:03.270
your players to the
world around them,

00:24:03.270 --> 00:24:05.510
including the businesses
restaurants, and shops

00:24:05.510 --> 00:24:07.330
around them.

00:24:07.330 --> 00:24:09.540
Some businesses will be
super eager to connect

00:24:09.540 --> 00:24:11.370
with your players,
and they'll want

00:24:11.370 --> 00:24:15.500
to be featured as coal mines or
collection points in your game.

00:24:15.500 --> 00:24:18.500
But other businesses,
like hospitals,

00:24:18.500 --> 00:24:22.510
won't appreciate crowds
near their entrances.

00:24:22.510 --> 00:24:25.090
To address this, you need
a centralized catalog

00:24:25.090 --> 00:24:27.700
of the businesses
around your users--

00:24:27.700 --> 00:24:30.827
all of them, all over the world.

00:24:30.827 --> 00:24:32.410
Pairing that with a
good understanding

00:24:32.410 --> 00:24:35.290
of player behavior will
enable you to evolve gameplay

00:24:35.290 --> 00:24:37.930
in a way that responds to
the player's environment

00:24:37.930 --> 00:24:40.300
and results in a game
that's fun for them

00:24:40.300 --> 00:24:43.330
and also fun for the
businesses around them.

00:24:43.330 --> 00:24:46.510
Again, this is the essence
of location intelligence--

00:24:46.510 --> 00:24:49.050
contextual signals about
the user's environment

00:24:49.050 --> 00:24:52.870
are used to enhance
their experience.

00:24:52.870 --> 00:24:55.080
OK, now let's talk
about the map itself--

00:24:55.080 --> 00:24:58.050
the best part-- the
land, water, roads,

00:24:58.050 --> 00:25:01.670
and buildings that form the
foundation of your game.

00:25:01.670 --> 00:25:03.620
In this case, the world
is your game of level,

00:25:03.620 --> 00:25:05.880
and the burden is on
you, the developer,

00:25:05.880 --> 00:25:10.040
to understand it in enough
detail to create a good game.

00:25:10.040 --> 00:25:13.040
If you accidentally
place a valuable object

00:25:13.040 --> 00:25:17.270
in the middle of an
industrial-scale garbage dump,

00:25:17.270 --> 00:25:20.060
someone is going to
find a way to get to it.

00:25:20.060 --> 00:25:22.580
Creating a game that's
both achievable and fun

00:25:22.580 --> 00:25:25.270
means knowing where you're
incentivizing players to go--

00:25:25.270 --> 00:25:28.150
again, at global scale.

00:25:28.150 --> 00:25:30.400
You'll need to understand
properties of the regions

00:25:30.400 --> 00:25:32.676
that each player is in.

00:25:32.676 --> 00:25:34.050
In the urban
environment, you can

00:25:34.050 --> 00:25:37.710
use a unique building
facade or building shape

00:25:37.710 --> 00:25:40.130
to orient a user.

00:25:40.130 --> 00:25:43.310
In the suburbs, you might use
parks, and in rural areas,

00:25:43.310 --> 00:25:46.370
you'll need to use terrain
and trails and paths

00:25:46.370 --> 00:25:48.200
to orient them.

00:25:48.200 --> 00:25:50.780
How do you design a single game
that's exciting everywhere,

00:25:50.780 --> 00:25:53.680
no matter where
your players are?

00:25:53.680 --> 00:25:56.590
In urban areas, you might use
3D building models or mark

00:25:56.590 --> 00:26:00.190
entrances to large businesses
or display indoor maps.

00:26:00.190 --> 00:26:02.650
And remember, all of this
needs to be performant enough

00:26:02.650 --> 00:26:05.610
to display on a smartphone.

00:26:05.610 --> 00:26:08.360
How should geography
influence your game?

00:26:08.360 --> 00:26:11.750
Should items perched atop a
hill or hidden in a dense forest

00:26:11.750 --> 00:26:15.530
be worth more than items placed
at a busy convenience store?

00:26:15.530 --> 00:26:16.810
What about weather?

00:26:16.810 --> 00:26:18.560
Should players who
venture out in the rain

00:26:18.560 --> 00:26:20.090
get more points
than those who live

00:26:20.090 --> 00:26:22.120
in the always-sunny Sydney?

00:26:22.120 --> 00:26:23.690
Probably.

00:26:23.690 --> 00:26:25.430
What about mode of travel?

00:26:25.430 --> 00:26:27.200
Should resource hunters
on public transit

00:26:27.200 --> 00:26:31.690
get more points than
resource hunters in cars?

00:26:31.690 --> 00:26:33.370
Or should the points
be player-specific?

00:26:33.370 --> 00:26:36.220
Like should you get more
points for exploring

00:26:36.220 --> 00:26:38.050
the world, further
away from your home

00:26:38.050 --> 00:26:41.590
or while you're on vacation or
more than 100 miles from where

00:26:41.590 --> 00:26:42.100
you work?

00:26:46.910 --> 00:26:49.040
Those were all static
features of the map,

00:26:49.040 --> 00:26:50.870
but, of course,
you'll probably want

00:26:50.870 --> 00:26:53.480
to make your game more
engaging by incorporating

00:26:53.480 --> 00:26:55.480
real-time elements, as well.

00:26:55.480 --> 00:26:58.160
And the possibilities
here are really endless,

00:26:58.160 --> 00:27:00.180
but I'll just illustrate a few.

00:27:00.180 --> 00:27:01.980
So for example,
as day transitions

00:27:01.980 --> 00:27:05.540
tonight, maybe the look and
feel of our game will shift.

00:27:05.540 --> 00:27:08.300
Maybe the game's night
mode incentivizes

00:27:08.300 --> 00:27:10.640
a totally different
set of behaviors,

00:27:10.640 --> 00:27:14.520
when most shops are closed,
some streets are deserted,

00:27:14.520 --> 00:27:17.540
and people are congregating
around a few central points

00:27:17.540 --> 00:27:19.550
of interest.

00:27:19.550 --> 00:27:21.650
What about real-time events?

00:27:21.650 --> 00:27:23.960
You could attach special
temporary resources

00:27:23.960 --> 00:27:29.030
to a parade or a game
or another event.

00:27:29.030 --> 00:27:30.350
What kind of events are there?

00:27:30.350 --> 00:27:32.776
A concert.

00:27:32.776 --> 00:27:35.400
This is a way to make your game
more social and more integrated

00:27:35.400 --> 00:27:37.369
with daily life.

00:27:37.369 --> 00:27:39.660
And, of course, you'll also
want to avoid sending users

00:27:39.660 --> 00:27:42.330
to businesses that are
closed and down streets that

00:27:42.330 --> 00:27:44.400
are deserted, and you
need real-time data

00:27:44.400 --> 00:27:47.910
to do both of those things.

00:27:47.910 --> 00:27:50.370
You'll also encounter lots
of interesting challenges

00:27:50.370 --> 00:27:53.670
when you deploy this
game in the wild.

00:27:53.670 --> 00:27:56.460
Users will try to
spoof their locations.

00:27:56.460 --> 00:27:59.400
They'll pretend to be in many
places at once and collect all

00:27:59.400 --> 00:28:03.390
possible resources, amassing
giant fortunes and building

00:28:03.390 --> 00:28:05.280
castles that cannot
be surpassed,

00:28:05.280 --> 00:28:09.030
and just ruining the
game for other players.

00:28:09.030 --> 00:28:14.160
You need to detect cheating and
prevent it at a global scale.

00:28:14.160 --> 00:28:16.690
You need to place treasures
carefully, and make sure

00:28:16.690 --> 00:28:18.690
that you aren't incentivizing
players to traipse

00:28:18.690 --> 00:28:21.190
into private property.

00:28:21.190 --> 00:28:24.690
You might also want to help
players avoid busy traffic

00:28:24.690 --> 00:28:29.070
and understand where it's safe,
appropriate, and even ideal

00:28:29.070 --> 00:28:33.060
to play games without having to
verify each location manually.

00:28:37.512 --> 00:28:39.720
I hope that example helped
highlight some of the ways

00:28:39.720 --> 00:28:43.980
that geodata, including location
intelligence, real-time data,

00:28:43.980 --> 00:28:46.380
and the map itself
can be used to design

00:28:46.380 --> 00:28:49.480
a next-generation
real-world game.

00:28:49.480 --> 00:28:51.970
Even this pretty
simple and very generic

00:28:51.970 --> 00:28:54.820
example illustrates that
there are endless ways

00:28:54.820 --> 00:28:59.290
to use geodata to make your game
safer, more social, and more

00:28:59.290 --> 00:29:01.940
immersive.

00:29:01.940 --> 00:29:04.550
And by the way, I think lots
of these apply to services

00:29:04.550 --> 00:29:07.050
outside of gaming, as well.

00:29:07.050 --> 00:29:09.840
All mobile developers are
pretty interested in making

00:29:09.840 --> 00:29:13.440
their services safer, more
social, and more immersive,

00:29:13.440 --> 00:29:15.450
and we think that
Google Maps data

00:29:15.450 --> 00:29:18.440
can help you do that at scale.

00:29:18.440 --> 00:29:19.850
When you design
around the world,

00:29:19.850 --> 00:29:23.330
you get an experience that's
unique and nuanced no matter

00:29:23.330 --> 00:29:28.239
where your users are, and
that's really powerful.

00:29:28.239 --> 00:29:30.280
And, of course, when you
deploy this in the wild,

00:29:30.280 --> 00:29:32.010
you need Cloud
Analytics to inform

00:29:32.010 --> 00:29:33.760
gameplay and Cloud
Infrastructure

00:29:33.760 --> 00:29:34.885
to support global rollouts.

00:29:38.750 --> 00:29:42.380
We're gearing up to see many
more real-world games released

00:29:42.380 --> 00:29:44.002
this year.

00:29:44.002 --> 00:29:45.710
Game developers are
beginning to approach

00:29:45.710 --> 00:29:47.780
us to understand
how they can conduct

00:29:47.780 --> 00:29:50.750
the real world to
gameplay, and how

00:29:50.750 --> 00:29:52.670
they can use location
intelligence to build

00:29:52.670 --> 00:29:55.200
more immersive experiences.

00:29:55.200 --> 00:29:57.200
We think Google Maps is
well-positioned to power

00:29:57.200 --> 00:30:00.410
that connection,
and we want to help.

00:30:00.410 --> 00:30:02.090
As games get more
sophisticated, we'll

00:30:02.090 --> 00:30:05.240
see them leverage real-world
data in new and inventive ways,

00:30:05.240 --> 00:30:08.330
including all of the
things I just talked about.

00:30:08.330 --> 00:30:10.100
Those things will then
become table stakes

00:30:10.100 --> 00:30:12.830
for the games that follow.

00:30:12.830 --> 00:30:15.670
We're excited to see developers
use the new technologies that I

00:30:15.670 --> 00:30:17.170
touched on.

00:30:17.170 --> 00:30:21.429
Location precision and powerful
smartphones are here to stay,

00:30:21.429 --> 00:30:23.470
and we're thrilled to see
developers combine them

00:30:23.470 --> 00:30:28.720
with the new and emerging types
of geodata that we can offer.

00:30:28.720 --> 00:30:30.420
So we want to work
with game experts

00:30:30.420 --> 00:30:34.570
to discover new
frontiers in this space.

00:30:34.570 --> 00:30:37.600
If you have ideas that combine
base maps with dynamic place

00:30:37.600 --> 00:30:41.690
information in your game or
application, come talk to us.

00:30:41.690 --> 00:30:43.570
We want to help developers
leverage metadata

00:30:43.570 --> 00:30:47.260
about the base map to understand
their players' environment

00:30:47.260 --> 00:30:49.960
or connect players to the
businesses around them

00:30:49.960 --> 00:30:52.780
or provide 3D models to
help them orient themselves.

00:30:55.310 --> 00:30:58.430
If you want to use aggregated
movement data or other location

00:30:58.430 --> 00:31:01.689
intelligence signals to drive
engagement, then let us know.

00:31:01.689 --> 00:31:03.230
We want to help
developers understand

00:31:03.230 --> 00:31:05.420
how their players are
moving in the world,

00:31:05.420 --> 00:31:08.662
and how their game is
ultimately being played.

00:31:08.662 --> 00:31:10.370
We also want to hear
your ideas about how

00:31:10.370 --> 00:31:12.860
to use data to stop
cheaters, and build

00:31:12.860 --> 00:31:17.550
fraud detection into your
games before you launch.

00:31:17.550 --> 00:31:19.650
If you have special
requirements around analytics

00:31:19.650 --> 00:31:22.840
and infrastructure,
definitely let us know.

00:31:22.840 --> 00:31:24.580
We're here to help
developers analyze

00:31:24.580 --> 00:31:28.450
player behavior to improve
the in-game experience.

00:31:28.450 --> 00:31:30.760
We can help crunch data
and deliver insights that

00:31:30.760 --> 00:31:32.945
improve your game in real time.

00:31:32.945 --> 00:31:35.445
We don't want you to have to
think about your infrastructure

00:31:35.445 --> 00:31:38.640
scaling, certainly, and we
want to see global rollouts

00:31:38.640 --> 00:31:43.720
taking place seamlessly
without downtime or anxiety.

00:31:43.720 --> 00:31:45.280
Finally, this one
gets its own slide

00:31:45.280 --> 00:31:47.960
because I think it's
really important.

00:31:47.960 --> 00:31:52.370
For a real-world game to go
viral, it has to go global,

00:31:52.370 --> 00:31:53.810
and our phenomenal
coverage makes

00:31:53.810 --> 00:31:57.630
that possible without
manual data collection.

00:31:57.630 --> 00:32:01.950
We can provide 3D building
coverage all over the world.

00:32:01.950 --> 00:32:06.630
Our Places dataset has
details on 100 million places

00:32:06.630 --> 00:32:09.420
all across the world.

00:32:09.420 --> 00:32:11.310
What if your game could
be played anywhere,

00:32:11.310 --> 00:32:15.750
without spending hours of
developers' time collecting

00:32:15.750 --> 00:32:18.390
and stitching together datasets?

00:32:18.390 --> 00:32:21.870
Believe us-- we know how
long that piece takes.

00:32:21.870 --> 00:32:24.120
Google Maps has been
working for over a decade

00:32:24.120 --> 00:32:26.580
to provide a comprehensive
model of the world

00:32:26.580 --> 00:32:28.854
with increasingly
dynamic information,

00:32:28.854 --> 00:32:31.020
and we're excited to see
how developers will combine

00:32:31.020 --> 00:32:33.990
the resources we provide
to make the next generation

00:32:33.990 --> 00:32:34.930
of real-world games.

00:32:37.660 --> 00:32:40.420
At Google Maps, we're
constantly working

00:32:40.420 --> 00:32:44.280
to make our model of the world
more useful to developers.

00:32:44.280 --> 00:32:47.250
13 years ago, we started with
a simple web interface that

00:32:47.250 --> 00:32:49.910
could be panned and zoomed.

00:32:49.910 --> 00:32:52.160
Today, we help developers
all over the world

00:32:52.160 --> 00:32:54.710
create experiences
that connect users

00:32:54.710 --> 00:32:56.480
to our increasingly
detailed model

00:32:56.480 --> 00:32:59.770
of the world in real time.

00:32:59.770 --> 00:33:02.250
We've already seen
mobile maps revolutionize

00:33:02.250 --> 00:33:05.700
industries like exercise
and ride sharing

00:33:05.700 --> 00:33:08.800
and the constant
hunt for good food.

00:33:08.800 --> 00:33:10.990
We're seeing more and
more mobile apps use

00:33:10.990 --> 00:33:16.250
location intelligence to draw
users in and keep them engaged.

00:33:16.250 --> 00:33:18.980
We're also seeing the
real world augment apps,

00:33:18.980 --> 00:33:21.840
and apps augment the real world.

00:33:21.840 --> 00:33:24.110
And as that happens,
the line is blurring

00:33:24.110 --> 00:33:28.020
between an immersive
mobile app and a game.

00:33:28.020 --> 00:33:29.900
We're excited by these
early examples that

00:33:29.900 --> 00:33:32.741
point to a paradigm
shift in gaming, as well,

00:33:32.741 --> 00:33:35.240
and we can't wait to see what
new innovations game designers

00:33:35.240 --> 00:33:37.730
will bring to the industry.

00:33:37.730 --> 00:33:40.240
We're sure the game
developers will use data

00:33:40.240 --> 00:33:42.040
in ways that make
us rethink what

00:33:42.040 --> 00:33:43.630
we need to know
about the real world

00:33:43.630 --> 00:33:45.430
to create a
compelling experience.

00:33:48.300 --> 00:33:50.220
So in summary, if
you're building

00:33:50.220 --> 00:33:52.530
a game that incorporates
elements of geodata,

00:33:52.530 --> 00:33:55.540
come talk to us and tell us
what you're trying to do.

00:33:55.540 --> 00:33:58.690
If you're building immersive
mobile experiences that

00:33:58.690 --> 00:34:02.630
incorporate real-world elements,
come talk to us, as well.

00:34:02.630 --> 00:34:05.440
And if you see applications
of next-generation geodata

00:34:05.440 --> 00:34:09.290
for your business outside
of gaming, come talk to us.

00:34:09.290 --> 00:34:12.770
My team and I will be at the
Maps experts area today and all

00:34:12.770 --> 00:34:13.760
of tomorrow--

00:34:13.760 --> 00:34:16.460
immediately following this
talk and also tomorrow--

00:34:16.460 --> 00:34:19.299
and I'm also going to invite
a few of my team members

00:34:19.299 --> 00:34:20.840
up to the side of
the stage right now

00:34:20.840 --> 00:34:22.760
to answer any questions
that you may have,

00:34:22.760 --> 00:34:25.050
so feel free to come forward.

00:34:25.050 --> 00:34:27.350
Thank you for your time,
and we hope to talk to you.

00:34:27.350 --> 00:34:31.000
[MUSIC PLAYING]

